   468	
   469	## §6 Querying
   470	
   471	### 6.1 The flat per-node projection
   472	
   473	`project : tree → #{[id tag & slots]}`, one row per distinct subtree:
   474	
   475	- `id` is the subtree's address (§4.3);
   476	- `tag` is the node's tag;
   477	- a `node` slot holds the child's address; a `nodes` slot holds a vector of
   478	  child addresses; every other slot holds its data unchanged.
   479	
   480	Because a tag has one arity in the tree, it has one arity in the projection
   481	(arity plus one for the id). Rows of one tag are homogeneous, so a
   482	where-pattern for a tag has exactly that tag's arity and no padding. Rows
   483	from many trees union into one relation without collision, because ids are
   484	addresses. Identical subtrees collapse to one row; an occurrence count is
   485	a count over parent slots or over the occurrence relation below, not over
   486	rows. The collapse is an identity use and is gated by §4.2.
   487	
   488	The §2.1 example projects to:
   489	
   490	```clojure
   491	#{[A :lambda [x] B]
   492	  [B :application C [D E] true]
   493	  [C :variable +]
   494	  [D :variable x]
   495	  [E :literal 1]}          ; A..E are :segment/sha256-… addresses
   496	```
   497	
   498	A second projection, `occurrences : tree → #{[root-address path
   499	node-address]}`, one row per place, is the join key between the flat rows
   500	and every occurrence-keyed side table of §2.5. It is computed by the same
   501	walk that computes `project`.
   502	
   503	### 6.2 Segment rows
   504	
   505	`project-segment : vector → [[pc tag & ops] …]`, the instruction vector with
   506	pc prepended. This relation is per segment: pcs collide across segments,
   507	and source scope belongs to the interpreter, never to a tuple slot
   508	(`datom.world.md:34`). A query over several segments takes them as
   509	separate sources (`:in $ $2 …`) or uses the **segment-qualified form**
   510	`[segment-address pc tag & ops]`, which adds one to every tag's arity
   511	uniformly and keeps per-tag arity fixed. Bare-pc rows from different
   512	segments are never unioned into one relation.
   513	
   514	### 6.3 `q` over both, and the mechanics it rests on
   515	
   516	`q` accepts these relations through `query/relation`
   517	(`src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc:153-158`), whose contents are an arbitrary
   518	mixed-dimensional tuple collection. A pattern clause is exact-arity
   519	positional unification: `eval-pattern-clause` (`query.cljc:894-907`) parses
   520	the pattern (`query.cljc:419-436`), and on the general path unifies every
   521	row through `unify-slots` (`query.cljc:799-809`), which first requires
   522	`tuple-shape-matches?` (`query.cljc:439-441`) and then unifies slot by
   523	slot. A final `& ?tail` binds the remaining slots as a vector and `& _`
   524	ignores them (`query.cljc:431-436`, `:807-808`). The 3-slot EAV fast path
   525	(`query.cljc:899`) engages only when the source carries a fact index, which
   526	`fact-relation` (`query.cljc:172-177`) and the datom views supply; the code
   527	relations never do, so every code pattern takes the general path by
   528	construction.
   529	
   530	Verified shapes, each of which is a rule a query author may rely on:
   531	
   532	- **Per-tag selection.** `[?id :variable ?name]` matches only arity-3 rows.
   533	- **Joins across arities.** `[?site :application ?op _ _] [?op :variable
   534	  ?f]` joins an arity-5 row to an arity-3 row on the address.
   535	- **Operand membership** goes through a predicate: `[(member? ?operands
   536	  ?x)]` with `member?` supplied under `:fns`; predicate arguments must
   537	  already be bound.
   538	- **Ledger as-of** (§8.6) and **ref-to-code joins** (§6.4) run in one
   539	  query, the ref pattern on the fast path and the code pattern on the
   540	  general path.
   541	
   542	Two gotchas are rules:
   543	
   544	- `match` (`query.cljc:520-528`) is constant-or-wildcard selection:
   545	  `slots-match?` (`query.cljc:444-449`) treats only `_`, nil, and the
   546	  internal free marker as wildcards (`query.cljc:409-411`), so a `?var`
   547	  written in a `match` pattern is compared as a constant and binds nothing.
   548	  Use `q` to bind.
   549	- `:fns` keys are quoted symbols: `eval-fn-clause` looks the clause head up
   550	  as a symbol (`query.cljc:1099`), and the builtins merge under the same
   551	  keys (`query.cljc:1436-1438`). `{:fns {'member? f}}`, as
   552	  `test/dao/space/stigmergy_test.clj:228` writes it.
   553	
   554	### 6.4 Refs joined by address
   555	
   556	A ref is a datom whose `v` is an address (§8). A name is an entity carrying
   557	`:yin/name`; joining refs to code is a join on the address value:
   558	
   559	```clojure
   560	;; every call site, in the tree currently named `my.ns/f`, whose operator is `+`
   561	[:find ?root ?path
   562	 :in $refs $code $occ
   563	 :where [$refs ?e :yin/name my.ns/f]          ; bind the name entity
   564	        [$refs ?e :yin/code ?root]            ; 3-slot, fast path, current view
   565	        [$code ?site :application ?op _ _]    ; general path
   566	        [$code ?op :variable +]
   567	        [$occ ?root ?path ?site]]             ; every place that content occurs in this tree
   568	```
   569	
   570	`$refs` is a `current` view (§8.6), so the name resolves to its current
   571	address. The result names places, not contents: the occurrence relation
   572	turns one shared site row into one result per place.
   573	
   574	### 6.5 What goes to the datom projection instead
   575	
   576	A tree also projects to datoms, `[e a v t m]` with tempids minted in
   577	preorder and one extra fact `[e :yin/address addr]` per node so the
   578	projection joins back to refs. This is the successor of `ast->datoms`
   579	(`v2.cljc:372-482`) and is the *projection path* of §7.1. It exists for
   580	three queries the flat rows deliberately do not serve:
   581	
   582	| Query | Why the datom projection |
   583	|---|---|
   584	| attribute-dimension queries with the attribute unbound, `[?e ?a ?v]` | a flat row has no attribute slot; "every fact about this node" is an EAV question |
   585	| `pull` and `entity-attrs` navigation (`query.cljc:715`, `:1481`) | they navigate entity → attribute → value and reverse attrs |
   586	| scale indexing under covered indexes (`dao.space.index`) | the covered-index publisher and `open-published!` are EAVT/AEVT/AVET/VAET over datoms (`query.cljc:452-476`) |
   587	
   588	The datom projection never becomes an identity: its `e` values are local,
   589	and the only durable name in it is the `:yin/address` value.
   590	
