   592	
   593	## §7 The VM around it
   594	
   595	### 7.1 Load paths
   596	
   597	Direct tuple loading is primary. A batch on the program stream is one
   598	canonical tree (AST medium) or one canonical instruction vector (code
   599	medium), and the evaluator's loader takes it as is:
   600	
   601	| Evaluator | Loader input | What loading does |
   602	|---|---|---|
   603	| walker | tree | validate (§7.4), set `:program` and `:control` to the tree; no conversion, the tuple is the node |
   604	| semantic | instruction vector | validate (§7.5), decode positional operands into the image (the `case` of `semantic.cljc:563-593` reading `(nth tuple i)` instead of attribute maps), store under `:code` with the address as the alias column UCF §7.3.4 requires |
   605	| semantic, AST medium | tree | `lower` (§5.2) then the row above; composed by the composition exactly as `linearize/ast-loader` is today (`linearize.cljc:269-286`, chosen in `src/cljc/yin/repl/core.cljc:63-68`) |
   606	
   607	Datom-batch loading is the projection path: a batch of `:yin/*` datoms is
   608	decoded to a tree by the successor of `datoms->ast` (`v2.cljc:494-559`)
   609	and then loaded as above; a batch of `:yin.code/*` datoms is decoded to a
   610	vector by UCF §7.3.4's projection rule and then loaded as above. The
   611	projection path exists for media that carry datoms and for the datom
   612	projection of §6.5; it is not a second loader and it runs the same
   613	validator (§7.2).
   614	
   615	### 7.2 Conformance: both paths, one validator, one image
   616	
   617	**This section supersedes the sentence in UCF §7.3.4 that names the
   618	projection path as the reference path.** Under the owner's ruling the
   619	direct path is primary and the reference; the projection path is derived
   620	from it. Everything else in §7.3.4 stands.
   621	
   622	Both paths invoke the same validator (§7.4 for trees, §7.5 for vectors)
   623	before anything is registered, and the conformance obligation has three
   624	parts:
   625	
   626	1. **Valid corpus, same result.** For every corpus program, loading the
   627	   tree directly and loading its datom projection yield the same walker
   628	   `:program` value; loading the instruction vector directly and loading
   629	   its datom projection yield the same image. Keyword store keys
   630	   (`parity_test.cljc:99`) are part of the corpus and must round-trip on
   631	   both paths.
   632	2. **Malformed input, same refusal.** For a published set of malformed
   633	   vectors, each violating one rule of §7.5, and malformed trees, each
   634	   violating one rule of §7.4, both paths refuse with the same defect
   635	   (rule and pc, or rule and path). A correctly hashed `[[:jump 9]]` is in
   636	   the set. Image equality on valid input does not establish this; the
   637	   malformed set does.
   638	3. **Several segments loaded.** Loading two or more segments through each
   639	   path yields the same `:code` map and the same address alias column, and
   640	   a second segment claiming a live local id fails identically on both.
   641	
   642	The obligation fails exactly when the tuple grammar and the datom schema
   643	have drifted apart, which is the property that makes it the VM's own test
   644	of this design.
   645	
   646	### 7.3 Validation is the tuple grammar
   647	
   648	A tuple loader validates before it decodes, and validation is the §2 table
   649	plus the structural rules a segment needs, nothing else. Both checkers
   650	mirror `code/well-formed?` (`code.cljc:155-180`): each returns nil or the
   651	first defect, rules run in order, and each rule may assume the earlier ones
   652	held.
   653	
   654	### 7.4 Tree rules
   655	
   656	Defects name a path (§2.5).
   657	
   658	| Rule | Defect when |
   659	|---|---|
   660	| `:tag` | the first element is not a tag of §2.3 |
   661	| `:arity` | the count differs from the tag's arity |
   662	| `:slot-kind` | a slot's value is not of the slot's kind: a `node` slot that is not a vector with a tag, a `nodes` slot that is not a vector of such, a `data` slot failing `plain-data?`, a `key` slot that is neither symbol nor keyword, a `bool` slot that is not `true`/`false`, a `syms` slot with a non-symbol |
   663	| `:saturation` | a saturated slot (§2.4) is nil |
   664	| `:encoder-domain` | a `data` slot's value contains a list (§4.2); this rule is removed when the type-preserving encoding lands |
   665	
   666	### 7.5 Vector rules
   667	
   668	Defects name a pc. Rules 1–4 of `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.6 (one segment,
   669	dense pcs, sorted by pc, refs resolve to entities) are satisfied by the
   670	vector form by construction and have no rule here; rules 5 and 6 and the
   671	UCF §7.3.4 checks are translated as follows.
   672	
   673	| Rule | Defect when |
   674	|---|---|
   675	| `:nonempty` | the vector has no instructions |
   676	| `:mnemonic` | the first element is not a mnemonic of `code/mnemonics` (`code.cljc:12-16`) |
   677	| `:arity` | the tuple's count differs from the mnemonic's arity in `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.4 as saturated by UCF §7.3.2 |
   678	| `:operand-kind` | an operand is not of its kind: `:const` and `:store-put` values fail `plain-data?`, a `:var` name is not a symbol, a `:closure` params is not a vector of symbols, a `:store-get`/`:store-put` key is neither symbol nor keyword, a `:ffi-call` op or `:resume` parked id is not a keyword, a `:gensym` prefix is not a string |
   679	| `:saturation` | a saturated operand (`:gensym` prefix, `:stream-make` buffer, `:call` tail?, `:ffi-call` argc) is nil |
   680	| `:target-bounds` | a `:jump`/`:branch-false` target or a `:closure` body is not an integer in `[0, length)` |
   681	| `:terminator` | the last instruction is not in `code/terminators` (`code.cljc:19-21`) |
   682	| `:argc` | a `:call`/`:ffi-call` argc is not a non-negative integer |
   683	| `:encoder-domain` | a `:const` or `:store-put` value contains a list (§4.2); removed with the encoding |
   684	
   685	The address check (the vector hashes to the address it claims) is UCF
   686	§7.3.4's and runs before these rules whenever an address is claimed; a
   687	correct hash is never structural validation. The failure outcome of both
   688	checkers on the UCF lowering path is `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the pc or
   689	path, as §7.3.4 already specifies; on a local load it is a load error
   690	naming the same.
   691	
   692	### 7.6 The hot loop never queries
   693	
   694	The walker dispatches on `(nth node 0)` and reads slots by position; the
   695	semantic VM runs `(aget code pc)` over an image decoded once at load
   696	(`yin.vm.semantic.md` §3.1). Neither touches a relation, a ref, an address
   697	index, or a projection while stepping. Projection and query are load-time
   698	and tooling-time operations. This is the rule that keeps
   699	`yin.vm.semantic.md` §6's cost model intact under this design: queryability
   700	is paid once at load, or by whoever asks, never per step.
   701	
   702	### 7.7 Dependency closure is Datalog over the tuples
   703	
   704	UCF §7.6.1 computes `:yin.k/requires` by a conservative fixed point over
   705	values, code, names, and modules. The **code part** of that fixed point is
   706	the queries below, run over the flat projection of every reachable tree
   707	and the segment-qualified rows (§6.2) of every reachable segment. The
   708	value, name-satisfaction, and module parts remain the walk §7.6.1
   709	specifies; these queries supply their inputs and do not replace them.
   710	
   711	Extraction over trees (`$ast` is the union of flat rows of the reachable
   712	trees):
   713	
   714	```clojure
   715	;; names, every one, not only free ones (UCF §7.6.1, Names)
   716	[:find ?name :in $ast :where [$ast _ :variable ?name]]
   717	
   718	;; store keys read or written by code
   719	[:find ?key :in $ast :where (or [$ast _ :vm/store-get ?key]
   720	                                [$ast _ :vm/store-put ?key _])]
   721	
   722	;; FFI ops
   723	[:find ?op :in $ast :where [$ast _ :dao.stream.apply/call ?op _]]
   724	
   725	;; parked ids named by resume
   726	[:find ?pid :in $ast :where [$ast _ :vm/resume ?pid _]]
   727	
   728	;; effect kinds raised by syntax itself: the tag is the effect kind
   729	[:find ?tag :in $ast
   730	 :where [$ast _ ?tag & _]
   731	        [(contains? #{:stream/make :stream/put :stream/cursor :stream/next
   732	                      :stream/close :vm/park :vm/resume :vm/gensym
   733	                      :vm/store-get :vm/store-put :vm/current-continuation} ?tag)]]
   734	```
   735	
   736	Extraction over segments (`$code` is the union of segment-qualified rows):
   737	
   738	```clojure
   739	[:find ?name :in $code :where [$code _ _ :var ?name]]
   740	[:find ?key  :in $code :where (or [$code _ _ :store-get ?key]
   741	                                  [$code _ _ :store-put ?key _])]
   742	[:find ?op   :in $code :where [$code _ _ :ffi-call ?op _]]
   743	[:find ?pid  :in $code :where [$code _ _ :resume ?pid]]
   744	[:find ?mn   :in $code
   745	 :where [$code _ _ ?mn & _]
   746	        [(contains? #{:stream-make :stream-put :stream-cursor :stream-next
   747	                      :stream-close :park :resume :gensym :store-get
   748	                      :store-put :current-continuation} ?mn)]]
   749	```
   750	
   751	Composition with the rest of §7.6.1, as rules:
   752	
   753	- **Names to callables.** Each extracted name that is not bound by a
   754	  carried environment or a store-slice key is a required primitive or
   755	  module export, checked by profile (UCF §7.5.2). The **effects of a
   756	  callable are read from its profile** (`:yin.k/effects`), never inferred
   757	  from the name; `yin/def` contributes `:vm/store-put` and `require`
   758	  contributes `:module/require` because their profiles say so. A name with
   759	  no profile at the emitter sets `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`.
   760	- **Parked ids.** Each extracted parked id must name a record in the
   761	  carried `:parked` slice (UCF §7.6.3); a missing one is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`
   762	  naming the id.
   763	- **Values.** Closures, streams, and cursors reachable from the frame, the
   764	  K frames, parked records, the store slice, and the pending wait
   765	  contribute segment addresses and stream identities as §7.6.1 specifies;
   766	  each newly reached address is fetched, projected, and its rows added to
   767	  `$ast` or `$code`, and the queries are re-run until no new address
   768	  appears.
   769	- **Modules.** A required module with an undeclared footprint sets
   770	  `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`, as §7.6.1 already rules.
   771	
   772	Discovery is `:complete` only when every reachable address was fetched
   773	and validated, every name resolved to a profile or a carried binding,
   774	every parked id resolved, and every module footprint was declared. It is
   775	`:blocked` when an address cannot be fetched and `:incomplete` otherwise.
   776	Recasting the walk as Datalog changes where the facts come from, not the
   777	conservatism of the closure, and it does not resolve the cross-activation
   778	name question §7.6.1 deliberately declines to guess at: every name is
   779	collected, whatever environment might bind it.
   780	

