   785	
   786	## §8 The ledger and refs
   787	
   788	### 8.1 Two notations, one relation
   789	
   790	The ledger is one logical relation with rows `[t op address ref]`, ruling
   791	6's shape: `t` is the ledger's clock, `op` is drawn from an open
   792	vocabulary, `address` is a content address, and `ref` is the row's other
   793	argument, a name entity or a second address. This document writes ledger
   794	facts in that notation when it speaks of the relation.
   795	
   796	The **persistent representation** of a row is datoms, and a datom is bound
   797	by `datom.md`'s contract: `e`, `t`, and `m` are stream-local, `e` is a
   798	non-negative integer, `m` is an integer, and `v` is the only slot that may
   799	hold a foreign reference (`datom.md:140-180`). The transactor enforces
   800	this: `local-datom?` (`src/cljc/dao/datom.cljc:42-58`) rejects a
   801	non-integer `e` or `m`, and `pad-datom`
   802	(`src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:74-90`) rejects a caller-supplied `t`
   803	and stamps its own. Therefore a content address never appears in `e`, an
   804	op keyword never appears in `m`, and `t` is never chosen by the writer.
   805	The mapping between notations is:
   806	
   807	| Logical slot | Persistent carrier |
   808	|---|---|
   809	| `t` | the datom `t` the transactor stamped |
   810	| `op` | for naming rows: the reserved validity marker in `m` (`:db/assert` 1, `:db/retract` 0); for every other op: the `:yin.ledger/op` value of a **ledger event entity** (§8.2) |
   811	| `address` | a `v` slot |
   812	| `ref` | for naming rows: the local name entity in `e`; for address-to-address rows: a second `v` slot of the same event entity |
   813	
   814	Anything written in the `[t op address ref]` notation below is a logical
   815	row; anything written as `[e a v t m]` is transactor input, with `e` a
   816	local integer or tempid, `t` nil at write time, and `m` a reserved marker.
   817	
   818	### 8.2 Ledger records and ledger events
   819	
   820	Every non-naming ledger fact is first a **ledger record**: a plain map,
   821	content-addressed by `segment-key`, stored in `dao.jing` like any object.
   822	Record shapes:
   823	
   824	| Op | Record | Meaning |
   825	|---|---|---|
   826	| `:derive` | `{:yin.ledger/op :derive :yin.ledger/input tree-addr :yin.ledger/output seg-addr :yin.ledger/function fn-name :yin.ledger/contract stamp}` | `output` was computed from `input` by `function` under `contract` (§5.2) |
   827	| `:expand` | `{:yin.ledger/op :expand :yin.ledger/input tree-in :yin.ledger/output tree-out :yin.ledger/macro lambda-addr :yin.ledger/attempt attempt-id}` | one expansion attempt; failures carry `:yin.ledger/error` instead of `:yin.ledger/output` (§8.4) |
   828	| `:publish` | `{:yin.ledger/op :publish :yin.ledger/subject occurrence-id :yin.ledger/output ucf-addr}` | a checkpoint occurrence was published as the UCF value at `output` (UCF §7.3.4) |
   829	
   830	A record is an object, so it is immutable, addressable, and verifiable,
   831	and it is what "the hash chain" is made of: a record names its input and
   832	output by address, and the record's own address is what the ledger
   833	publishes. Walking the chain is following record addresses; a code object
   834	never has to know its predecessor.
   835	
   836	A record enters the ledger as a **ledger event entity**, a local entity
   837	(id ≥ `dao.datom/first-user-id`, `datom.cljc:27-40`) with the record's
   838	fields as attributes and the record's address as one more:
   839	
   840	```clojure
   841	[[:db/add ev :yin.ledger/op       :derive]
   842	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/input    tree-addr]
   843	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/output   seg-addr]
   844	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/function :yin.vm/lower]
   845	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/contract "v2/0"]
   846	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/record   record-addr]]     ; ev is a tempid; m defaults to :db/assert
   847	```
   848	
   849	Event operation and fact validity are separate by construction. The op is
   850	a namespaced keyword in a `v` slot, globally meaningful without a
   851	reserved id, which is `datom.md`'s own rule for built-ins that live in `a`
   852	and `v` (`datom.md:214-217`); the datoms of the event carry the ordinary
   853	`:db/assert` marker and fold as ordinary facts. No reserved `m` id is
   854	allocated, because reserved ids have one global meaning everywhere
   855	(`datom.md:200-212`) and a composition cannot mint one. Where a
   856	composition wants an event to be the provenance of a naming fact, the
   857	naming datom's `m` names the event entity through the established
   858	metadata-entity mechanism, an id ≥ 16 carrying `:db/op` and provenance
   859	(`datom.cljc:2-7`, `datom.md:172-180`; the transactor's map-`m` input at
   860	`src/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc:15-29`). A fact whose `m` names a
   861	metadata entity is live under the current fold, which removes only
   862	`:db/retract` markers (`query.cljc:76-103`); a validity op other than
   863	assert or retract is expressed by the reserved marker, never by the
   864	event, so the fold needs no knowledge of the open vocabulary. Interpreting
   865	a metadata entity's `:db/op` for Datomic-visible validity is the
   866	prerequisite `dao.space.schema.datomic.md` §4 names and is outside this
   867	design.
   868	
   869	The vocabulary is open: a composition that needs a new op declares a new
   870	keyword and a record shape. A reader dispatches on the ops it understands
   871	(`[?ev :yin.ledger/op :derive]`) and does not see the others, which is a
   872	property of the query it writes, not of the fold.
   873	
   874	### 8.3 Rows of the one relation
   875	
   876	| Logical row | Persistent form |
   877	|---|---|
   878	| `[t :assert tree-addr name]` — a name refers to a tree | `[:db/add name :yin/code tree-addr]` |
   879	| `[t :retract tree-addr name]` — the name no longer does | `[:db/retract name :yin/code tree-addr]`, followed by the new assertion |
   880	| `[t :derive seg-addr tree-addr]` — a segment was lowered from a tree | a `:derive` event entity (§8.2); direction: `input` is the tree, `output` is the segment |
   881	| `[t :assert seg-addr k-occurrence]` — a segment is the address a continuation names | `[:db/add occ :yin.k/segment seg-addr]` on the local occurrence entity |
   882	| `[t :expand tree-out tree-in]` — a tree is the expansion of another | an `:expand` event entity (§8.4) |
   883	| `[t :assert lambda-addr name]` under `:yin.macro/definition` | not a ledger row at all: macro-ness is an occurrence-bound declaration beside the tree (§8.5); the ledger records only the expander's harvest result, `[:db/add name :yin.macro/definition lambda-addr]`, for queries |
   884	| `[t :publish ucf-addr occurrence]` — a checkpoint occurrence was published | a `:publish` event entity; custody stays `dao.lease.md`'s vocabulary unchanged (UCF §7.7.2), keyed by the same local occurrence entity |
   885	| `[t :assert tree-addr manifest]` — a module manifest pins a tree | `[:db/add manifest :yin.module/exports tree-addr]` |
   886	
   887	`:yin.code/derived-from` therefore keeps its name as a query alias for
   888	"the `:yin.ledger/input` of a `:derive` event whose output is this
   889	address" and is no longer an attribute of the segment (`v2.cljc:327`,
   890	`linearize.cljc:212`).
   891	
   892	### 8.4 Expansion events keep their identity
   893	
   894	`yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1 records one event per attempt, successful or not,
   895	including repeated identical expansions, and links the event to its output
   896	(`yin.vm.macro.md:666-709`). This design keeps that contract: the
   897	`:expand` event entity **is** that event, one per attempt, and the address
   898	rows are attributes of it, not a replacement for it:
   899	
   900	```clojure
   901	[[:db/add ev :yin.ledger/op        :expand]
   902	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/input     tree-in-addr]        ; the tree containing the call
   903	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/call      [tree-in-addr path]]  ; the occurrence of the call (§2.5)
   904	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/output    tree-out-addr]       ; on success
   905	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/macro     lambda-addr]         ; the macro's content
   906	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/attempt   n]                   ; per-expander attempt counter
   907	 [:db/add ev :yin/source-batch     batch-id]            ; unchanged from §4.1
   908	 [:db/add ev :yin/error            {...}]               ; on failure, unchanged
   909	 [:db/add ev :yin/timestamp        ctx-t]]              ; unchanged
   910	```
   911	
   912	`:yin/expansion-root`, `:yin/source-call`, `:yin/source-node`, and
   913	`:yin/macro` of §4.1 are carried by `:yin.ledger/output`,
   914	`:yin.ledger/call`, and `:yin.ledger/macro` with addresses and occurrence
   915	keys as values; the other event attributes are unchanged. Two attempts
   916	that expand identical input to identical output are two events with equal
   917	address attributes and distinct attempt numbers, so nothing is conflated.
   918	Because the values are addresses and occurrence keys, the cross-media
   919	identity problem of `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.2 does not arise for them: the
   920	same tree has the same address on `program-in`, `program-out`, and the
   921	log. The event entity itself is local to the log medium, as it is today.
   922	
   923	### 8.5 Macro declarations travel beside the tree
   924	
   925	The `:macro?` flag is outside the canonical tree (§2.5). What replaces it
   926	is a **declaration fact bound to an occurrence**, produced by the frontend
   927	and consumed by the expander at admission. The composition between them
   928	is:
   929	
   930	```clojure
   931	{:yin/code          <tree>                          ; canonical, addressable
   932	 :yin/declarations  #{[path :yin.macro/definition] …}}  ; occurrence-bound facts about this tree
   933	```
   934	
   935	Rules:
   936	
   937	- **Binding.** A declaration `[path :yin.macro/definition]` names the
   938	  `:application` at `path` in `:yin/code`; that node must be
   939	  `(yin/def <literal sym> <lambda>)`, i.e. operator `[:variable yin/def]`,
   940	  first operand a `:literal` symbol, second operand a `:lambda`. A
   941	  declaration whose path does not resolve to such a node is an admission
   942	  failure, `{:kind :malformed-input :reason :stray-macro-declaration}`,
   943	  the successor of `yin.vm.macro.md` §3.1 step 1's `:stray-macro-lambda`.
   944	- **Ordering.** Harvest order is preorder of paths, which is batch order
   945	  in the map form (`yin.vm.macro.md:242-249`); last wins.
   946	- **Missing fact.** A `(yin/def sym v)` with no declaration at its path is
   947	  a plain definition. If `sym` is currently a macro, the plain definition
   948	  removes it, exactly as `yin.vm.macro.md` §3.1 step 2 rules.
   949	- **Scope.** Harvest is syntactic and whole-batch, as today; a declaration
   950	  in an unexecuted branch is in force for the batch. Declarations are per
   951	  batch and never persist beyond the harvest that consumed them; the
   952	  harvested store is the expander's state.
   953	- **Redefinition.** Two batches that carry identical trees differ in
   954	  behaviour only through their declarations, which is the intended
   955	  separation: content is what the program is; being a macro is what this
   956	  occurrence of a definition is *for*.
   957	
   958	This replacement protocol, not the expander itself, is the blocker
   959	(§10.5): tuple evaluation without macros needs none of it, and the v2
   960	expander does not exist yet.
   961	
   962	### 8.6 As-of queries are ledger views
   963	
   964	Current value of a name uses the implemented current-state interpreter,
   965	which resolves the greatest `t` per `[e a v]` and then removes retractions
   966	(`query/current`, `query.cljc:204-212`; the fold `current-state-seq`,
   967	`query.cljc:76-103`):
   968	
   969	```clojure
   970	(q '[:find ?addr . :in $ ?name :where [?e :yin/name ?name] [?e :yin/code ?addr]]
   971	   (query/current refs) 'my.ns/f)
   972	```
   973	
   974	Value of a name as of `?t` is the same view with its as-of argument, which
   975	bounds visible datoms to `t ≤ ?t` before folding
   976	(`query/current` two-arity, `query.cljc:210-212`; `bound-datoms`,
   977	`query.cljc:68-73`):
   978	
   979	```clojure
   980	(q '[:find ?addr . :in $ ?name :where [?e :yin/name ?name] [?e :yin/code ?addr]]
   981	   (query/current refs t) 'my.ns/f)
   982	```
   983	
   984	A hand-written negation over raw d5 rows is not the contract and is not
   985	used: "any retraction before `?t`" wrongly removes an address that was
   986	retracted and later reasserted, whereas the fold's latest-event-wins per
   987	`[e a v]` keeps it. A query that must inspect raw rows selects the latest
   988	event per `[e a v]` with `max ?t` first and interprets that row's `m`
   989	second.
   990	
   991	Provenance walks are joins over event entities:
   992	
   993	```clojure
   994	;; what was this segment lowered from, and what did that expand from
   995	[:find ?src
   996	 :in $ ?seg
   997	 :where [?d :yin.ledger/op :derive] [?d :yin.ledger/output ?seg] [?d :yin.ledger/input ?tree]
   998	        [?x :yin.ledger/op :expand] [?x :yin.ledger/output ?tree] [?x :yin.ledger/input ?src]]
   999	```
  1000	
