  1052	### 9.2 The observer lane: an integration item to scope, not a solved detail
  1053	
  1054	Program input reaches an evaluator through
  1055	`dao.stream.observer/run-on-stream` (`src/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc:217-247`),
  1056	which is shape-agnostic: it hands each observed batch to a
  1057	composition-supplied `load`. The per-evaluator loaders are chosen in
  1058	`yin.repl.core/program-loaders` (`repl/v2/core.cljc:63-68`) and today
  1059	accept datom batches; the REPL's own eval path still converts a
  1060	semantic-VM AST through `ast->datoms` before it travels
  1061	(`repl/v2/core.cljc:444-449`). Two things about the tuple lane are
  1062	**unverified**:
  1063	
  1064	- **The batch shape.** Whether one batch is one tree, one `{:yin/code
  1065	  :yin/declarations}` value (§8.5), one vector, or a collection of them is
  1066	  a medium contract this design has not exercised end to end; §7.1 assumes
  1067	  one canonical value per batch. The shape must be fixed with the REPL
  1068	  shell and the `yin.vm.stream-observer` tests before the loaders are
  1069	  switched.
  1070	- **The program-input predicate.** `engine/executable-program-datom?`
  1071	  (`engine.cljc:511-513`) decides what counts as program input by testing
  1072	  the attribute namespace of an `[e a v t m]` row, and
  1073	  `append-program-datoms` (`engine.cljc:590-612`) builds on it. A tuple
  1074	  batch has no attribute slot. A repository search on 2026-09-14 found no
  1075	  caller of the v2 `append-program-datoms` beyond its own overload, so a
  1076	  tuple counterpart is not presumed necessary; whether any live
  1077	  composition reaches the predicate is what the implementation phase
  1078	  establishes before deciding.
  1079	
  1080	Both are integration items to scope in the implementation phase.
  1081	
  1082	### 9.3 What stays on datoms, and why
  1083	
  1084	- **The ledger, every ref, and every ledger event** (§8): mutability,
  1085	  history, provenance, and custody are datom properties, and the
  1086	  transactor is what possesses them.
  1087	- **`dao.space.index` and published covered indexes**: they index datoms,
  1088	  and the scale path of §6.5 goes through them.
  1089	- **The macro expander's event log** (§8.4): events are entities with
  1090	  attributes, one per attempt; only their values changed kind.
  1091	- **The datom projection of code** (§6.5): kept for the three query classes
  1092	  it alone serves, regenerable from the object at any time.
  1093	- **Execution traces** (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §3.6): state over time is a
  1094	  datom stream by design and is untouched.
  1095	
