   255	## §3 The two boundary calls
   256	
   257	### 3.1 `:vm/store-update` is excluded from the canonical grammar
   258	
   259	The node carries a host function in its `:fn` field and applies it inside
   260	the syntax (`ast_walker.cljc:410-415`). It is excluded on two grounds, and
   261	they are sufficient: a canonical object admits no host value (§2.2,
   262	`data`), and the node is absent from every persistent and portable form
   263	already, since the codec has no arm and throws on it (`v2.cljc:413-478`),
   264	the linearizer lists it as unsupported (`linearize.cljc:19-22`), the
   265	semantic VM has no opcode for it, and no test constructs one (a search of
   266	`test/` for the node type on 2026-09-14 found none). Its one reachable path
   267	is the walker's map-input arm, which survives only as long as that path
   268	(§9.1).
   269	
   270	The orchestrator's alternative, a `:fn` slot restricted to a named
   271	primitive symbol resolved and profile-checked per UCF §7.5.2, is not
   272	adopted: it would re-admit a node that no persistent form carries, for a
   273	convenience no corpus program uses.
   274	
   275	A program that needs the behaviour writes it as an application of the
   276	`yin/def` primitive (`v2.cljc:128`) over a `:vm/store-get` operand:
   277	`(yin/def k (f (vm/store-get k) args…))`. That rewrite is equivalent to
   278	the arm **only under stated conditions**, and this document does not claim
   279	it as a general semantics-preserving migration:
   280	
   281	- `yin/def` and `f` resolve through `resolve-var`'s precedence, env → store
   282	  → primitives → modules (`src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:46-58`), so the
   283	  rewrite is equivalent only when neither name is shadowed at the site;
   284	- the arm stores whatever `(apply f current args)` returns as data, while
   285	  an application interprets an effect-shaped return
   286	  (`ast_walker.cljc:184-188`, `semantic.cljc:206-220`), so the rewrite is
   287	  equivalent only when `f` returns plain data.
   288	
   332	### 4.2 The transitional encoder is inherited, and it gates every identity use
   333	
   334	`segment-key` hashes an order-normalized `pr-str` (`jing.cljc:67-71`,
   335	`:203-214`) and `order-normalize` coerces every sequential value to a vector
   336	(`jing.cljc:63`). Therefore `[:literal [1 2]]` and `[:literal (1 2)]`
   337	address identically today although they are different values. This is
   338	`dao.jing.md`'s first open item (*Canonical encoding*), inherited unchanged,
   339	and it is an acceptance blocker of this design (§10.1).
   340	
   341	The gate is on the **use**, not on the lifetime of the store: any operation
   342	that treats equal addresses as equal values is unsound until the collision
   343	is closed. That includes a process-local address-keyed cache, the collapse
   344	of identical subtrees into one flat-projection row (§6.1), the DAG sharing
   345	of §4.4, a `dao.jing` read that resolves an address to a literal object,
   346	and every ledger row. Until the pinned type-preserving encoding lands,
   347	each such use is admissible only over an **explicitly validated restricted
   348	value domain**: a checker over the tree that rejects any `data` slot whose
   349	value contains a list, so that within the admitted domain the transitional
   350	encoder is injective. The checker is a rule of §7.4's validator and is
   351	removed, not relaxed, when the encoding lands. When it lands, every address
   352	minted under this design changes with every other `dao.jing` address, the
   353	dependency UCF §7.3.2 already accepts.
