   737	## §7.6 Dependency closure and context (blocker 3)
   738	
   739	### 7.6.1 Declared, not hand-written — and discovered conservatively
   740	
   741	A resumer must be able to decide *before lowering* whether it can run the
   742	value. `:yin.k/requires` carries that decision's inputs, and every set in it
   743	is **computed by a conservative fixed point**, not hand-written and not
   744	guessed from the current environment:
   745	
   746	```clojure
   747	:yin.k/requires
   748	{:yin.k/segments        #{:segment/sha256-3f1a… :segment/sha256-9c02…}  ; every address in frame, k, closures, values, parked
   749	 :yin.k/primitives      {sym → profile, §7.5.2}                        ; every {:yin.k/primitive …} encoded, plus every free :var
   750	 :yin.k/modules         {my.mod :segment/sha256-…}                     ; module name → manifest address
   751	 :yin.k/effects         #{:stream/next :stream/put :module/require}    ; effect kinds the segments can raise
   752	 :yin.k/ffi-ops         #{:op/add}                                     ; :yin.code/ffi-op values present
   753	 :yin.k/streams         #{<identity> …}                                ; logical-stream identities referenced anywhere
   754	 :yin.k/cursor-profiles #{:dao.stream/file-v1}                         ; transports that must accept kept cursors, §7.4.3
   755	 :yin.k/discovery       :complete}                                     ; :complete | :incomplete | :blocked — computed, §7.6.5
   756	```
   757	
   758	The fixed point starts from the frame, its K frames, the parked records of
   759	§7.6.3, the store slice, and the pending wait, and repeats until nothing new
   760	is reachable:
   761	
   762	- **Values:** every encoded closure contributes its segment address and its
   763	  captured env's values; every stream and cursor marker contributes a stream
   764	  identity; every `:yin.k/primitive` marker contributes a name.
   765	- **Code:** every reachable segment's instructions are walked — as datoms
   766	  where the emitter holds the batch, as canonical tuples where only a vector
   767	  was fetched (§7.3.4) — including **`:store-get` and `:store-put`
   768	  operands**, which name store
   769	  keys directly and which the first draft missed entirely; every `:var`
   770	  name; every effect op (`:stream-*`, `:ffi-call`, and the effects of
   771	  `:effectful`-class primitives the walk finds); every `:resume` operand
   772	  (a parked id, §7.6.3). Walking a *newly fetched* segment continues the
   773	  fixed point with its own contents.
   774	- **Names:** a `:var` name is satisfied if it is bound in any environment
   775	  the value carries, any store key the slice includes, a required primitive,
   776	  or a required module. The walk collects *every* `:var` name in reachable
   777	  code, not merely those unbound in the top frame's E — E is per-activation
   778	  and closures capture different ones, so resolution against "the current
   779	  environment" is exactly the guess this computation exists not to make. An
   780	  unsatisfied name after the fixpoint is a required primitive (checked by
   781	  profile, §7.5.2) or a `:yin.k/unsatisfied` entry.
   782	- **Modules:** a module's manifest declares its exported primitive profiles
   783	  and its **store footprint** (store keys and effect kinds its handlers may
   784	  touch). Until manifests carry footprints, any required module whose
   785	  footprint is undeclared sets `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete` — the honest
   786	  statement that closure could not be computed, never a silent
   787	  under-approximation. A module effect handler may read store keys the
   788	  static walk cannot see; a composition may supply extra slice keys
   789	  explicitly, and the value records that it did.
   790	
   791	Because the computation is over data, the same query answers "what does this
   792	program need?" for a segment that has never run. And because discovery can
   793	*fail to terminate successfully* — a missing segment cannot be walked, an
   794	undeclared footprint cannot be closed — the result distinguishes
   795	`:complete` from `:incomplete` from `:blocked` (§7.6.5); a fixed point that
   796	could not finish is reported as such, not passed off as a satisfied closure.
   797	
   798	### 7.6.2 The store slice, restored as an isolated store
   799	
   800	The store S holds `def` results, stream handles, cursor entries, and the FFI
   801	pair (§4.1). None of it is global in the UCF sense; it is the emitter's. A
   802	continuation carries the **reachable slice**: every store key the fixed
   803	point of §7.6.1 can name — every `:var` name in reachable code, every
   804	`:store-get`/`:store-put` operand, every stream/cursor cell — with values
   805	encoded by §7.5:
