   666	### 4.1 The expansion event
   667	
   668	Every attempt, successful or not, produces one event; so does every batch
   669	rejected at admission. Events and the `m`-tagged output are appended to the
   670	**log** medium, when the expander has one; `program-out` receives only
   671	program.
   672	
   673	```
   674	[ev :yin/type           :macro-expand-event  t default-op]
   675	[ev :yin/source-batch   <ctx :t>             t default-op]   ; which source batch; always present
   676	[ev :yin/source-call    <call-eid>           t default-op]   ; VALUE, undeclared: the call's eid as it appeared in the source batch, qualified by :yin/source-batch; only on an initial (non-nested) expansion
   677	[ev :yin/source-node    <node-eid>           t default-op]   ; DECLARED REF, log-local: the call node in the log copy of the enclosing expansion's output; only on a nested expansion
   678	[ev :yin/macro-name     <sym>                t default-op]   ; when the operator was a :variable; absent for an inline macro lambda or an admission failure
   679	[ev :yin/macro          <lambda-eid>         t default-op]   ; only when durable (§4.2)
   680	[ev :yin/expansion-root <root'>              t default-op]   ; on success; names the LOG copy
   681	[ev :yin/error          {:kind ...}          t default-op]   ; on failure; plain data naming entities, never carrying input values
   682	[ev :yin/timestamp      <ctx :t>             t default-op]   ; logical, never a host clock
   683	```
   684	
   685	An **admission-failure event** is the same entity with only `:yin/type`,
   686	`:yin/source-batch`, `:yin/error`, and `:yin/timestamp`: the batch had no
   687	usable call or macro to name. It is allocated from `:alloc`'s watermark
   688	(which needs no index), so a corrupt batch still produces exactly one
   689	plain-data event, advances `:ctx` once, and is retried through the same
   690	per-medium staging as any other log payload — never regenerated.
   691	
   692	The log copy of expansion output carries `m = ev`; log copies of unchanged
   693	operands and ancestors carry `m = default-op` (re-parenting, not macro
   694	output). **`program-out` datoms all carry `default-op`**: `m = ev` would be
   695	a cross-medium ref (§4.2). An initial expansion's `:yin/source-call` is a
   696	value, not a ref: the call's eid as it appeared in the source batch,
   697	qualified by `:yin/source-batch`, which no index or transactor resolves. A
   698	nested expansion carries `:yin/source-node` instead — a declared ref to a
   699	node in the log copy of the enclosing expansion's output — which is how the
   700	chain source → event → root → event → root is walked for generated syntax.
   701	A successful expansion carries exactly one of the two; an admission failure
   702	carries neither. Only `:yin/source-node` is a declared ref. The names:
   703	`:yin/source-call` keeps its existing name in this narrowed value role so
   704	that existing log data is not invalidated by the split; `:yin/source-node`
   705	is new and is named for what it points at — a node of the log copy, the
   706	term this document already uses for AST nodes there — because "call" would
   707	mislead: a nested expansion names the outer expansion's output node, not a
   708	call site in any source batch.
   709	"Every datom this expansion produced" is `[?d _ _ _ ?ev]` on the log.
