   210	
   211	**Occurrences.** Two identical subtrees at different places in one tree
   212	have one content address (§4.3), so an address cannot name a place. A
   213	place is named by an **occurrence key** `[root-address path]`, where
   214	`root-address` is the address of the whole tree and `path` is the
   215	structural path from the root: a vector of slot indices, with an index into
   216	a `nodes` slot written as a pair `[slot i]`. `[]` is the root;
   217	`[2 [1 0]]` is the first operand of the root's second slot. A path is a
   218	pure function of the tree, so it is stable across media and hosts. Every
   219	side table and every provenance link that must distinguish places is keyed
   220	by occurrence, never by node address alone.
   221	
   222	| Side table | Row | Keeper |
   223	|---|---|---|
   224	| source positions | `[root-address path file line col]` | the frontend that parsed the file |
   225	| frontend metadata | `[root-address path key value]` | the frontend |
   226	| batch identity | `[root-address batch-id]`: the program-stream batch (`:yin/source-batch` in `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1's sense) that carried the tree | the composition that observed it |
   227	| instruction provenance | `[segment-address pc root-address path]` | the lowering (§5.3) |
   228	| macro declarations | `[root-address path]`: the definition at `path` is a macro definition (§8.5) | the frontend, consumed by the expander |
